{# Podcast TTS Prompt Templates
  These Jinja2 templates define prompts for emotion tagging.

  Modes:
  - batch_emotion_prompt: Single-call processing of all dialogue lines (fastest, consistent context)
  - single_emotion_prompt: Individual line processing without context (fallback)
  - single_emotion_with_context_prompt: Individual line processing with previous lines for emotional continuity
#}

{# Batch emotion tagging prompt - processes all dialogue lines in single LLM call
   This is the recommended mode for production use (~20x faster than single mode)
   Maintains emotional consistency across all lines since LLM sees full context
#}
{% macro batch_emotion_prompt(dialogue_lines) -%}
Analyze the EMOTION for each of these {{ dialogue_lines|length }} podcast dialogue lines.

For each line, return an 8-float emotion vector in this order:
[happy, angry, sad, afraid, disgusted, melancholic, surprised, calm]

Each value should be between 0.0 and 1.0. Complex emotions can have multiple non-zero values.

{% for line in dialogue_lines %}
Line {{ loop.index }}: {{ line.speaker }}{% if line.direction %} {{ line.direction }}{% endif %}
Text: "{{ line.text }}"

{% endfor -%}

Respond with ONLY a JSON array of {{ dialogue_lines|length }} emotion vectors:
[
  [0.1, 0.8, 0.3, 0.2, 0.0, 0.1, 0.4, 0.0],
  ...
]

Nothing else. No explanation.
{%- endmacro %}

{# Single-line emotion tagging prompt WITHOUT context (fastest individual mode)
   Use this for parallel mode or when you don't need emotional continuity
#}
{% macro single_emotion_prompt(line) -%}
Analyze the EMOTION of this podcast dialogue line and respond with ONLY an 8-float list.

Speaker: {{ line.speaker }}{% if line.direction %} Direction: {{ line.direction }}{% endif %}
Text: "{{ line.text }}"

Return 8 floats for emotion intensity in this order:
[happy, angry, sad, afraid, disgusted, melancholic, surprised, calm]

Each value should be between 0.0 and 1.0. Complex emotions can have multiple non-zero values.

Respond with ONLY the 8 floats, like: [0.1, 0.8, 0.3, 0.2, 0.0, 0.1, 0.4, 0.0]
Nothing else. No explanation.
{%- endmacro %}

{# Single-line emotion tagging prompt WITH context (sequential mode)
   Use this when you need emotional continuity through the conversation.
   Includes previous dialogue lines to maintain consistent emotional tone.
#}
{% macro single_emotion_with_context_prompt(line, previous_lines=[]) -%}
Analyze the EMOTION of this podcast dialogue line and respond with ONLY an 8-float list.

{% if previous_lines %}
CONTEXT (previous lines for emotional continuity):
{% for prev_line in previous_lines %}
- {{ prev_line.speaker }}: "{{ prev_line.text }}"{% if prev_line.emotion_vector %} (emotion: {{ prev_line.emotion_vector }}){% endif %}
{% endfor %}

{% endif %}
CURRENT LINE:
Speaker: {{ line.speaker }}{% if line.direction %} Direction: {{ line.direction }}{% endif %}
Text: "{{ line.text }}"

Return 8 floats for emotion intensity in this order:
[happy, angry, sad, afraid, disgusted, melancholic, surprised, calm]

Each value should be between 0.0 and 1.0. Complex emotions can have multiple non-zero values.

{% if previous_lines %}
Consider the emotional flow from the previous lines to maintain continuity.
{% endif %}

Respond with ONLY the 8 floats, like: [0.1, 0.8, 0.3, 0.2, 0.0, 0.1, 0.4, 0.0]
Nothing else. No explanation.
{%- endmacro %}
